<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    What the VS Code extension asks about a rule set, answered by the Rulealize this repository
    already takes rather than by anything written in TypeScript. A language server on standard
    input and output: whether a rule set compiles against a folder of vocabularies, the rules it
    has and what a specification has, what a screen's XAML asks a rule set to give, how a value somebody chose
    to try is written into a test design, and what changed of the specification and the screen
    beyond binding them to the rules.

    The same program is the command line an application's folder is checked with — whether its
    rule set compiles, whether its specification and rules agree, whether its screen does what its test
    design says — so that what an agent is told to run is what the editor runs. And what the
    editor runs to show a situation of the test design as the application's window: show; and what
    a change to the rules did to the design, as Ruledger's diff answers it: changes. And what the
    editor runs while a screen is designed on its own window: design. An application
    reaches it as a local tool, rulealize-studio, which packing writes to feed/ beside the libraries
    while none of them is on nuget.org.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <RootNamespace>RulealizeStudio.Server</RootNamespace>
    <PackAsTool>true</PackAsTool>
    <ToolCommandName>rulealize-studio</ToolCommandName>
    <Description>What the RulealizeStudio.Avalonia extension asks about a rule set, a screen and a specification, as a language server; and the same questions from an application's folder: rulealize-studio check, agree and replay; show, a situation of the test design as pictures of the application's window; changes, what a change to the rules did to the test design; and design, the screen drawn from its XAML as written and changed by edits to the text.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1605;CS1591</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Rulealize" Version="0.12.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    What a screen asks of a rule set is told apart from what the model has of its own by the
    binding layer's members, and a rule set's name is matched to the one XAML binds the way the
    generator makes it: by the generator's own code, compiled here too rather than written twice.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\RulealizeStudio.Binding\RulealizeStudio.Binding.csproj" />
    <Compile Include="..\RulealizeStudio.Generator\Name.cs" Link="Name.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The replay stands an application's screen in each state its test design names, and presses the
    controls, with no window server: the hosting library's Replay, on Avalonia's headless platform.
    Showing a situation draws the window there with Skia, as the application draws itself, and
    takes a picture of it. Designing the screen loads its XAML as it is written now, at run time,
    since nothing is built for each change.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\RulealizeStudio.Hosting\RulealizeStudio.Hosting.csproj" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Headless" Version="11.3.11" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Skia" Version="11.3.11" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Markup.Xaml.Loader" Version="11.3.11" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    What a change to the rules did is Ruledger's account: its diff, asked for as values and said
    here by the routes that reach each situation it names, never worked out here from two designs.
    The design itself still reaches an application as the file the walk writes, committed beside
    its rule set; this is the walk and the diff, called where they are needed.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Ruledger" Version="1.1.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
